## Modeling a Deck of Cards

A standard playing card has two independent dimensions: suit and rank. Both are enums.

### The Suit Enum

```xiom
enum Suit {
  Hearts,
  Diamonds,
  Clubs,
  Spades,
}
```

### The Rank Enum

```xiom
enum Rank {
  Ace,
  Two,
  Three,
  Four,
  Five,
  Six,
  Seven,
  Eight,
  Nine,
  Ten,
  Jack,
  Queen,
  King,
}
```

### The Card Struct

```xiom
type Card = {
  suit: Suit;
  rank: Rank;
}
```

### Creating Cards

```xiom
let ace_spades = Card{ suit: Suit.Spades; rank: Rank.Ace; };
let king_hearts = Card{ suit: Suit.Hearts; rank: Rank.King; };
let two_clubs = Card{ suit: Suit.Clubs; rank: Rank.Two; };
```

### Describing a Card

Convert the rank to a display name, then build a full description:

```xiom
fn rank_name(r: Rank) -> Str {
  match r {
    Ace => { return "Ace"; }
    Two => { return "2"; }
    Jack => { return "Jack"; }
    Queen => { return "Queen"; }
    King => { return "King"; }
    _ => { return "?"; }
  }
}

fn suit_symbol(s: Suit) -> Str {
  match s {
    Hearts => { return "\u2665"; }
    Diamonds => { return "\u2666"; }
    Clubs => { return "\u2663"; }
    Spades => { return "\u2660"; }
  }
}

fn Card.describe(self) -> Str {
  return rank_name(rank) + " of " + suit_symbol(suit);
}
```

### Building a Hand

```xiom
let hand = Card{
  suit: Suit.Hearts;
  rank: Rank.Ace;
};
```

### Checking for Pairs

```xiom
fn is_pair(a: Card, b: Card) -> Bool {
  return a.rank == b.rank;
}
```

A pair means same rank, regardless of suit.

